<div class="wrap two"><article class="prose">
<h2>How depreciation is estimated here</h2>
<p>Each line uses straight-line depreciation: age divided by expected life, up to the cap you choose. With <em>Depreciate materials only</em> checked, the labor share you enter is left undepreciated. Uncheck it to see what the first check would look like if the whole line were depreciated.</p>
<h2>Why the labor column is there</h2>
<p>Arkansas courts have addressed whether labor can be depreciated when calculating actual cash value, in decisions including <em>Adams v. Cameron Mutual</em> (2013) and <em>Shelter Mutual v. Goodner</em> (2015). The law can change, so treat the comparison as illustrative and talk to an Arkansas attorney if the question matters to your claim. Our guide on <a href="/appraisal-guides/labor-depreciation-arkansas-acv/">labor depreciation in Arkansas</a> explains more.</p>
<h2>First check and holdback</h2>
<p>The first check is ACV minus the deductible. The holdback is recoverable depreciation, paid after repairs are completed within the policy’s time limit. ACV-only policies don’t pay the holdback.</p>
<h2>Questions about this tool</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>What expected life should I use?<svg class="chev" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Common figures are 25–30 years for laminated shingles, 10 years for interior paint and 15–25 years for many floor coverings. Your carrier’s estimate may list its own.</p></div></details><details><summary>Why is there a depreciation cap?<svg class="chev" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Many estimates cap depreciation so an item never drops to zero value. Check what your estimate used.</p></div></details><details><summary>Is this how my carrier calculated it?<svg class="chev" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Not necessarily. Carriers use their own methods and condition adjustments. Use this to ask informed questions.</p></div></details></div>
